
                     3Dfx VooDoo device driver for Mesa 2.4



This software is distributed under the terms of the GNU Library
General Public License, see the LICENSE.TXT file for details.

What do you need ?

	- A PC with a 3Dfx Voodoo Graphics or Voodoo Rush based board
	  (Monster 3D, R3D, Obsidian, Stingray 128/3D, etc.);

	- The Mesa Library 2.4;

	- The 3Dfx Glide library 2.3 or later for your OS (the 2.4 works fine);

	- A compiler supported by the Glide library (Micro$oft VC++ (tested),
	  Watcom (tested), GCC for Linux (tested), etc.);

	- A lot of patience, this is an alpha release.

	- It's nice to have two monitors- one for your normal graphics
	  card and one for your 3Dfx card.  If something goes wrong with
	  an application using the 3Dfx hardware you can still see your
	  normal screen in order to recover.


Tested on:

	Windows 95 - David Bucciarelli
	Windows NT - Henri Fousse
	MS-DOS
	Linux - Daryll Strauss, Brian Paul, David Bucciarelli


What is able to do ?

	- It is able accelerate points, lines and polygon with flat
	  shading, gouraud shading, Z-buffer, texture mapping. There is also
          the support for rendering in a window (with a slow trick for the
	  Voodoo Graphics (available only for Linux) and at full speed with
	  the Voodoo Rush chipset).


How to compile for MS Windows:

	For the MSVC++:
	- The glide2x.lib and the texus.lib have to be in the default MSVC++
	  lib directory;

	- The Glide headers have to be in the default MSVC++ include directory;

	- Edit the Mesa-2.4/mesafx.bat file and change the
	  "set MSVC=h:\winapps\msdev"
	  to point to the directory where you have installed the MSVC++;

	- Go to the directory Mesa-2.4 and run the mesafx.bat;

	- The script will compile everything (Mesa-2.4/lib/fxMesa.lib,
	  Mesa-2.4/lib/fxTk.lib, Mesa-2.4/lib/fxGLUT.lib and Voodoo demos);

	- At the end, you will be in the Mesa-2.4/3Dfx/demos directory;

	- Try some demo (fire.exe, teapot.exe, etc.) in order to check if
	  everything is OK;

	- I have checked this procedure only on my PC, I hope that it will
	  work with any MSVC++ installation;

	- I think that you can easy change the Makefile.fx files in order
	  to work with other kind of compilers;

	- To discover how open the 3Dfx screen, read the sources under
	  the Mesa-2.4/3Dfx/demos directory. You can use the fxTk emulator for
	  old programs or the Henri's port of the GLUT 3.3 library. If you
	  are going to write new programs use the fxGLUT library.
	  This version of the driver include also the Diego Picciani's wgl
	  emulator.  It can be very useful in order to buid the Mesa Library
	  as a DLL that is "compatible" with the Opengl32.dll.


How to compile for Linux:

	- You'll need the Glide library and headers.  Mesa expects:
		/usr/local/glide/include/*.h        // all the Glide headers
		/usr/local/glide/lib/libglide2x.so
		/usr/local/glide/lib/libtexus.so

	  If your Glide libraries and headers are in a different directory
	  you'll have to modify the Mesa-config file.

	- Unpack the MesaLib-2.4.tar.gz and MesaDemos-2.4.tar.gz archives;

	- If you're going to use a newer Mesa/Glide driver than v0.18 then
          unpack the new driver archive over the Mesa directory.

	- There is an small error in the Glide texus.h: two C++ comments (//);
	  You have to translate the comments in C (/*  */) by hand;

	- In the Mesa-2.4 directory type "make linux-glide"

	- Compilation _should_ finish without errors;

	- Set your LD_LIBRARY_PATH environment variable so that the
	  libglide2x.so and Mesa library files can be found.  For example:
		setenv LD_LIBRARY_PATH "/usr/local/glide/lib:/SOMEDIR/Mesa/lib"

	- You'll have to run Glide-based programs as root or set the suid
	  bit on executables;

        - Install the GLUT 3.5 library (this library is required by some demo).
	  Several GLUT 3.5 demos work fine with the Voodoo driver but you have
	  to modify the Makefiles in order to link everything also with the
	  glide and the texus library.

	- The GLUT library works thanks to a trick.
	  So you need X11 and some attention (don't move the mouse, don't use
	  the mouse buttons, click once time at the startup if you are using
	  interactive window placement, don't use autofocus);

	- In the directory Mesa-2.4/3Dfx/demos type "make"

	- Try the demo programs !


A Linux/3Dfx/X hack:

	The Mesa 2.4 is able to run the same executable in 3 differents
	configuration:

	1) X11/GLX or (if the X11/GLX isn't available) Mesa X11 driver;

	2) There's a hack which allows 3Dfx rendering into an
	X window (sort of).  The idea is we use the 3Dfx hardware to do
	rendering then copy the image from the 3Dfx frame buffer into an
	X window when the SwapBuffers() function is called.  The problem
	with this idea is it's slow.  The image must be copied from the
	3Dfx frame buffer to main memory then copied into the X window.

	Anyway, here's how to enable this hack:
	   1. Start your X server in 16 bpp mode.
	   2. Set the following environment variables:
		export MESA_GLX_FX="window"	// to enable window rendering
		export SST_VGA_PASS=1	// to stop video signal switching
		export SST_NOSHUTDOWN=1	// to stop video signal switching
	      OR
		setenv MESA_GLX_FX window
		setenv SST_VGA_PASS 1
		setenv SST_NOSHUTDOWN 1
	   3. You must use double buffering.
	   4. The program (or the toolkit which it uses) must use GLX.
	   5. Link with -lglide2x
	   6. Be sure you're _NOT_ using the 3Dfx/Glide version of glaux/
		gltk.  Use the regular GLX version (for example with the
		standard GLUT).

	3) Full screen with the Voodoo frame buffer.
	Set the following environment variables:

		export MESA_GLX_FX = "fullscreen"
	      OR
		setenv MESA_GLX_FX fullscreen

	Remember that the standard GLUT library works thanks to a trick.
	So you need X11 and some attention (don't move the mouse, don't use
	the mouse buttons, click once time at the startup if you are using
	interactive window placement, don't use autofocus);

	This "3-way" support works only if you use the the GLX interface
	(for example through the standard GLUT library). Otherwise, if you use
	directly the Voodoo driver interface (for example trough the
	3Dfx/Glide version of glaux/gltk), you will get only the full
	screen/full speed support.


Useful Glide Environment Variables:

	To disable the 3Dfx logo, set the FX_GLIDE_NO_SPLASH variable.
	To disable video signal switching:
		setenv SST_VGA_PASS 1
		setenv SST_NOSHUTDOWN


Know BUGS, problems and Hints:

	- maximum texture size: 256x256 (this is an hardware limit);

	- the texture memory is never released;

	- only GL_ALPHA, GL_LUMINANCE, GL_LUMINANCE_ALPHA, GL_RGB and GL_RGBA
	  texture channels (unsigned char) supported;

	- only one context supported;

        - use the glBindTexture extension (standard in OpenGL 1.1) for texture
	  mapping (the old way: glTexImage inside a display list, download
	  the texture map each time that you call the display list !!!);

	- use triangles and quads strip: they are a LOT faster than sparse
	  triangles and quads;

	- the alpha buffer doesn't yet works but you can use all the glBlend
	  functions that don't use the alpha buffer (all functions without
	  the DST_ALPHA);

	- very simple Bitmap support (but the GLUT and Tk font functions work
	  fine);

	- color index mode not implemented.

	- you can enable the 8bit textures feature using the following driver
	  function just before the texture definitions:

	  #ifdef FX
		  fxMesaTextureUsePalette(1);
	  #endif

	  All textures declared as GL_RGB will be stored using an ad hoc
	  palette with 256 entry and with an 8bit per pixel texture memory
	  usage (again the default 16 bit). The driver and the Texus library
	  have a lot of preprocessing to do (quantization, dithering, etc.)
	  in order to translate the GL_RGB texture to the 8bit format, so
	  don't be surprised if the startup of your program is slow
	  (downloading 35 large textures can requires more than 30 sec !).


Did you find a lot BUGs and problems ? Good, send me an email.


